[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048375}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234255}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Using Common Factors and Quadratic Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "23dd4876-d6d3-50c1-a5c6-9d5d709220d3" :step/math-academy-id 18056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139645 :tutorial/content "To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $2x^3-2x$.\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $2x^3-2x$ factors into $2x(x+1)(x-1)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "85714b2f-9623-52ab-aa32-df1827f75877" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3366 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bb62a766-8bbc-56b7-a5a5-1db2c882c43d" :step/math-academy-id 18735 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139293}}] :activity/title "Further Factoring of Polynomials Using GCFs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138638}]}
